00:00:04.477 --> 00:00:06.060
RAY TSANG: In this
video, you'll learn

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.950
how to quickly adopt
Google Cloud platform

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.180
services with Springboard
framework using Spring Cloud

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.080
GCP.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.540
GCP engineers have been working
closely with the Spring teams

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.640
to produce idiomatic
integrations

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.400
for many of our GCP services.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.760
Let's see how you can
use Cloud Spanner,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.200
add distributed
tracing to Cloud Trace,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.870
send logs to Cloud Logging
with a Spring Boot application.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.509
First, create a new application
using Spring Initializer

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.600
at start.spring.io.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.490
In the bottom, you
can add dependencies

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:40.780
to add capabilities to your
application at GCP support.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.680
Generate the application and
open it in your favorite IDE.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.720
You can use Spring Data
with Cloud Spanner,

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.690
so you can take advantage
of Spring Data features,

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.140
such as object relational
mapping, crowd repository,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.390
inventing, and auditing.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.080
First, add the Spring Cloud
GCP Cloud Spanner starter.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.030
Create a POJO for object
relational mapping.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.860
Annotate the POJO
with Cloud Spanner

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.540
annotations to configure a
table mapping and primary keys.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.120
Rather than creating your
own data access object,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.190
you can use Spring
Data's repository

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.510
to automatically generate and
implement cloud operations

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.770
to Cloud Spanner.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.380
In application.properties,
configure the Cloud Spanner

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.320
instance ID and database
ID to connect to.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.030
You can then use the repository
for basic cloud operations.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.250
But you can also
enhance the repository

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.730
with additional finder
methods or custom queries.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.250
Spring Data will
automatically infer the query

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.280
from the name of the
method, or use a query

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.940
that you provide, using
the query annotation.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.060
Finally, rather than creating
your own RestController

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.840
by hand, you can also use
Spring Data REST repository

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.240
to automatically
create and expose

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.310
a RESTful service
that can perform

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.220
cloud operations against
the Cloud Spanner database.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.310
It's important to understand
the topology of your distributed

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.150
system.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.070
You can accomplish
this through the use

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:13.690
of distributive tracing.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.390
You can use Spring
Cloud GCP Trace

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.920
Starter to add the
distributive tracing capability

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.450
to your service.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.710
It uses Spring Cloud
Sleuth underneath,

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.160
so it's native to
Springboard applications.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.310
And they will automatically
capture and propagate

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.610
the trace ID, the span ID.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.500
And it will automatically
configure Spring Cloud Sleuth

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.540
to send traces to Cloud Trace

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:39.410
By default, Spring Cloud Sleuth
will only sample and report

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.720
10% of the requests.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.880
You can adjust the
sampling probability.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.030
For this demo, were
adjusted to 100%.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.970
And this is all it takes
to add distributed tracing

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.480
to send traces to Cloud Trace.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.210
Similarly, to send log
messages to Cloud Logging,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.240
simply add the Spring
Cloud GCP Logging Starter.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.850
Then configure the standard
logback configuration

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.130
to use Cloud Logging appendix.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.780
There are two
appendix you can use.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.340
If you're running
on premise, you

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:10.220
can send log entries to the API.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.010
Or if you're running
in GKE, you can

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:17.810
use the JSON appender to produce
JSON format log entries that's

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.760
understood by GKE

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.430
In both cases, you do not
need to change any code.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.800
Simply use the
existing appenders.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.010
And most importantly, the
trace context information

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.800
will be automatically
added to the log entry,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.950
so that later, you can
correlate the trace graph

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.070
with the corresponding
log messages.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.150
Let's go take a look.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.070
In the Trace console,
we can clearly

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.920
see the trace associated
with a request

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.720
but also, its subsequent course.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.720
Check the show logs checkbox.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.887
And the related log
messages are also displayed.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.170
With Spring Cloud
GCP, you can quickly

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.380
create services or
migrate existing services

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.270
to utilize GCP.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.540
You can use Spring data with
Cloud Spanner, Firestore,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.590
and Data Store.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.290
You can add distributed
tracing and centralize logging

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:13.948
without adding custom code.

00:04:13.948 --> 00:04:14.990
And there's so much more.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

